The story of David and Goliath comes from the Hebrew Bible / Old Testament, in 1 Samuel 17.

## Short Version

The Israelites and the Philistines were at war. The Philistines had a giant warrior named Goliath who challenged the Israelites every day:

> “Choose a man to fight me!”

Everyone was terrified — including King Saul’s soldiers.

David was a young shepherd boy, not a soldier. He came to the battlefield only to bring food to his brothers. When he heard Goliath insulting Israel and God, David volunteered to fight him.

King Saul offered David armor and weapons, but David refused because they were too heavy and unfamiliar. Instead, David carried:

* a sling
* five smooth stones
* his shepherd’s staff

Goliath laughed when he saw the small shepherd boy approaching.

David said that Goliath came with sword and spear, but he came “in the name of the Lord.”

Then David put a stone in his sling, swung it, and struck Goliath in the forehead. Goliath fell to the ground. David then used Goliath’s own sword to kill him.

The Philistine army fled, and Israel won the battle.

## Main Themes

* Courage over size and strength
* Faith and confidence
* The underdog defeating a stronger opponent
* Using skill and intelligence instead of heavy weapons

## Why It’s Famous

The phrase **“David vs. Goliath”** is now used everywhere to describe:

* a weak person defeating a powerful opponent
* small companies beating huge corporations
* underdog sports victories

## Jerusalem Connection

Later, David became king and captured Jerusalem, making it the capital of ancient Israel. He is traditionally associated with the city more than almost any other biblical figure.

## Historical Note

Scholars debate how much of the story is literal history versus legendary tradition. But the story has had enormous influence on religion, literature, art, and culture for thousands of years.
